  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Planning the Layout**\n\nI\'m now focusing on designing the system\'s layout. First, I\'m examining the requirements, particularly for the customer management module. I\'m noting the need for full CRUD operations with validation, including unique email checks and fuzzy matching for duplicates. The system will also implement \"logical deletion,\" meaning data will be flagged as inactive instead of being permanently removed.\n\n\n**Refining Status Indicators**\n\nI\'m now detailing the customer status indicators. I\'ll use badges: green for active, yellow for debt, red for overdue, and gray for inactive. Each individual profile will display data, payment history, appointments, and debt status. I\'m also planning mass CSV imports with column mapping, preview, and error reports, plus CSV/Excel exports. The search function will be real-time based on name, surname, or email.\n\n\n**Detailing Schedule and Payments**\n\nI\'m now detailing the scheduling module. I\'ll display both fixed and flexible client lists. Fixed assignments will check slot availability and schedule conflicts. I\'ll implement an automatic waitlist for full slots, and a tool to recover missed fixed appointments. I will also have an editable maximum capacity without losing assignments. Then, I am developing the payments module, where I will define manual entry, and multiple payment methods. I\'ll also implement CSV import for virtual wallets with automatic mapping and duplicate detection, monthly reports, and overdue alerts.\n\n\n**Implementing Delinquency Control**\n\nI\'m now implementing the delinquency control module. I will set a rule: if a payment isn\'t received by the 5th at 23:59 (Argentina time), the customer is marked as delinquent. I envision a daily Supabase Edge Function simulation triggered by an interactive button. This will display the execution process, live updating states based on current date/timezone. This displays the Edge Function logic directly to the user! The table will show name, plan, last paid month, days overdue, and amount owed. I will implement quick filters for delinquency status, plus a quick action to register payments directly from the table. Key Performance Indicators will include total delinquents, delinquency percentage, and total outstanding debt.\nI\'m starting the development of the main dashboard with cards showing active clients, delinquent percentage, real monthly income, expected income, collection percentage, and average occupation. I plan line graphs for income over six months, bar graphs for shift occupancy, and pie charts for clients by plan. The alert panel is next on the roadmap.\n\n\n**Forecasting Income Streams**\n\nI\'m now projecting income for the next four months based on 100%, 75%, and 50% occupancy. I\'ll visualize this with plan distribution data including client count, prices, and subtotal. I will also compare installed capacity to sold capacity and highlight underutilized and saturated appointment slots.\n\n\\n\\n\n\n**Securing Access Levels**\n\nNow I\'m detailing authentication and security, specifically Admin (full access) and Operator (limited view) roles. I\'ll include a role switcher for easy evaluation of both, especially for evaluating security criteria. I am also implementing an audit log to monitor actions in real time for accountability. I am moving towards a modular React structure with defined types.\n\n\n**Structuring the Project**\n\nI\'m now focusing on organizing the project files. I\'m building `/src/useGymState.ts` or a React Context for persistent state management via `localStorage`.  I\'ll start with `/src/GymContext.tsx`, wrapping the whole app and providing all the necessary state arrays and methods to cover all the CRUD actions. I\'m taking the time to carefully plan how to build the required components!\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will now build `/src/GymContext.tsx`, which serves as our state engine, simulating a live Supabase connection with reactive, real-time updates and standard JSON persistence in browser local storage. This file integrates the logic for checking unique emails, fuzzy matching for duplicates, logic for assigning/unassigning fixed slots, waitlists, absences/recovery, processing monthly billing, and logging auditorial actions."
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 8
      nanos: 31924066
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
