  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing the Architecture**\n\nOkay, I\'m quite impressed by the existing setup; it seems robust. I\'ve begun to carefully analyze the new user requests. Specifically, I\'m considering the implications of incorporating two distinct \"TURNERAS\", one for fixed vacancies accessible only to administrators and professors. The architecture\'s adaptability is now the current focus.\n\n\n**Clarifying User Requirements**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the implications of the user\'s intent to remove user type restrictions and the introduction of fixed and variable slots. The core question is: how to structure the 3-day-per-week allotment, ensuring 2 fixed and 1 variable slot for every user, given that they want to remove any distinction between user types? I\'m evaluating data structures to optimally handle this unified model.\n\n\n**Verifying Data Storage**\n\nI\'m now validating the data storage for variable shifts. The \"Turnera de Tiempo Real\" must allow users to modify their `turno_variable`, which is a single shift ID. I\'m verifying that this system integrates with fixed assignments. The capacity is determined by the sum of fixed and variable slots. The current focus is ensuring the data model accurately reflects the fixed versus variable shift assignments.\n\n\n**Validating Access Logic**\n\nI\'ve confirmed the design adheres to access specifications, with administrators and professors having exclusive access to the Fixed Turnera. The Real-Time Turnera accurately reflects the sum of fixed assignments plus variable shifts, with variable shifts not impacting the Fixed Turnera\'s vacancy count. The ability of clients to modify their `turno_variable` within the Real-Time Turnera is also confirmed. I\'m now testing the role switcher for client simulation.\n\n\n**Implementing Client Portal**\n\nI\'m now implementing the \"Socio (Client)\" role in `RoleSwitcher`. This allows me to simulate the client experience directly. The crucial addition is a selection mechanism for a specific client to view their personalized panel. Upon selection, the layout transforms into a dedicated Client Portal. I am working on the Client Portal now, and it will contain access to a WhatsApp link and a list of the client\'s payments.\n\n\n**Refining Data Structures**\n\nI\'ve updated `src/types.ts` to include the `turno_variable?: string;` field for the `Cliente` interface, allowing users to modify their variable shifts in the Real-Time Turnera. This addition is crucial for the implementation. The current focus is ensuring this change seamlessly integrates with existing data structures. I\'m now verifying the changes and their implications on existing functionality.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will edit `src/types.ts` to add the `turno_variable?: string` field to the `Cliente` model, which will store the ID of the client\'s single variable shift."
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 9
      nanos: 481999100
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
