  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Developing Google Auth Functions**\n\nI\'m currently working on implementing `signInWithGoogle` and `signOutGoogle` functions, placing them strategically above the `borrarHistorial` function. I\'ve begun to flesh out the `signInWithGoogle` function, initially focusing on cleaning the email input and preparing for further implementation details. I also began to consider the parameters that the function will accept, for example email, nameName, and picture.\n\n\n**Assigning User Roles**\n\nI\'ve added the role assignment logic. The script detects user roles based on email addresses. Certain emails are hardcoded to \"ADMIN\" or \"OPERATOR\" roles. If the email doesn\'t match, it searches existing \"SOCIO\" users. If no match is found, the system registers a new \"SOCIO\" with default values derived from the provided name. The role assignment is working.\n\n\n**Refining Client Creation Logic**\n\nI\'ve refined the client creation process within Google authentication. Now, upon a successful Google sign-in, the system checks if the user\'s email already exists in the \"SOCIO\" list. If not, it creates a new \"SOCIO\" entry with default values including `p-3d`, `activo:true`, and zero `deuda_acumulada`, setting the `ultimo_mes_pagado` to the current month. The new client\'s default values have also been set up, including the creation date, and the inclusion into an update array. I then call to `setClientes` to update, and also update `localStorage`. The function sets up the default user and logs the user\'s login. I\'m also ensuring the user\'s role is correctly set and that the audit log is updated with relevant session data.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I am going to implement the `signInWithGoogle` and `signOutGoogle` methods and expose them through the Context Provider. This will allow any component in the application to log in or log out users."
