  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Considering Google Sign-In**\n\nI\'m making headway on implementing Google Sign-In. I realized the client-side Vite SPA makes integrating the Google Identity Services (GSI) client library possible. This means no server is needed; the client handles the loading of `https://accounts.google.com/gsi/client`.\n\n\n**Validating Google Integration**\n\nI\'ve validated the approach. Using Google Identity Services, I can asynchronously load the GSI client, initialize the One Tap/Sign-In client, render the official button, and handle the JWT credential callback entirely in the browser. Decoding the front-end JWT lets me get the Google profile data. It uses the official Google Sign-In SDK, and on clicking the button, a Google login popup appears for a seamless experience.\n\n\n**Exploring Client-Side Integration**\n\nI\'m now diving deeper into the technical aspects of client-side integration. I\'ve realized we can load the Google 3P Client Library using a script tag and initialize it. We also need to render the button. A significant concern is the Google Client ID. We could give instructions on how to set one up, offer a default ID, or allow the user to input or configure it. I\'m exploring the options.\n\n\n**Clarifying OAuth Intent**\n\nI\'ve realized the user\'s need is standard Google Sign-In for app authentication, not Google Workspace integration, which simplifies things. Now, I will look at the best way to prompt the user to get started!\n\n\n**Evaluating Firebase Auth**\n\nI\'m now weighing the Firebase Auth option. The current setup doesn\'t have Firebase configured, so that\'s a hurdle. While Google Identity Services seems viable for standard Google Sign-In with local storage, Firebase might become necessary with a full data store. The distinction between Google Workspace access (which needs `set_up_oauth`) and basic authentication, which needs Google Identity or Firebase Auth, is becoming clearer. I\'m checking for Firebase config files.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will read `.env.example` to see if there are any existing OAuth environment variables defined."
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 9
      nanos: 490743160
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
