  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Integrating Google SignIn**\n\nI\'m making good progress! I\'ve been examining the current GoogleSignIn component. It has a demo account option and manual email input, but needs real Google integration. I\'m exploring loading the official Google Identity Services button into `/src/components/GoogleSignIn.tsx`. This should provide a more authentic experience.\n\n\n**Assigning Google Client ID**\n\nI\'m now focusing on assigning the Google Client ID. I\'ve considered several approaches, including using a default ID like `45527719759-abcdefg.apps.googleusercontent.com` if one isn\'t provided. My current plan is to prioritize finding a Client ID within the workspace, then gracefully handling the absence of a defined ID, perhaps by requesting one or allowing the user to input it. I\'m exploring initializing Google login if the library loads.\n\n\n**Implementing Google Sign-In**\n\nI\'ve made a breakthrough! I\'m now implementing the official Google Sign-In button. I\'m rendering a `div` and initializing the Google Identity Services with a fallback Client ID if the environment variable isn\'t set. I\'m writing the `handleCredentialResponse` callback, which will decode the JWT and then call `signInWithGoogle` using the parsed email, name and picture.\n\n\n**Addressing Dynamic URLs**\n\nI\'ve realized a critical point: the ever-changing nature of the development container\'s URL creates a problem for Google OAuth. Hardcoding a Client ID won\'t work due to mismatched Authorized Javascript Origins. To fix this, I propose a world-class developer/user experience. I\'ll include the official Google Sign-In button, utilizing the `VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID` from the environment. Plus, I will add a config button.\n\n\n**Enhancing Configuration Flow**\n\nI\'m now focused on refining the user experience for Client ID configuration. My brilliant plan involves an elegant accordion/modal allowing the user to input their customized Client ID, which will be stored in localStorage for persistence. This ensures genuine Google Sign-In with minimal friction. Also, I\'ll keep the quick bypass accounts as an auxiliary collapsible section distinct from the main flow. This provides a balance between authentic GSI integration and the ability to verify roles in a sandbox environment. I\'m excited because this implementation perfectly addresses both concerns.\n\n\n**Implementing Local Storage**\n\nI\'ve realized a key enhancement. By storing the Google Client ID in `localStorage` under the key `\'gym_google_client_id\'`, I can load it directly in the authentication initializer. This enables seamless, native Google Sign-In as soon as Tob\303\255as adds his app\'s domain to the GCP Credentials and inputs the Client ID. I\'m double-checking the implementation specifics in `GoogleSignIn.tsx` now. My plan is to use a `useEffect` hook to ensure the `window.google` object is loaded, then initialize the Google button inside a specific DOM container. I\'m going to write a very error-proof `useEffect`.\n\n\n**Refining Client ID Handling**\n\nI\'m now refining the handling of the Google Client ID. I\'ve introduced a `googleClientLoaded` state variable and a `customClientId` state. The latter is initialized from `localStorage` or `VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID`, defaulting to a local development Client ID. I\'ve crafted a `useEffect` hook to initialize Google Identity Services only when the `window.google` object is available. It includes an interval for robustness. The initialization now renders the Google button within a dedicated container. The `callback` within the `initialize` method decodes the JWT and calls `signInWithGoogle` if successful.\n\n\n**Updating Google Button**\n\nI\'ve realized I need to re-initialize the Google button if the `customClientId` updates. I\'ve also incorporated error handling during Google Identity Services initialization. I\'m reviewing the implementation of `decodeJwt` for robustness, ensuring it can handle various JWT structures. I am adding the re-initialization of the button to a `useEffect` hook.\n\n\n**Displaying URLs for Configuration**\n\nI\'m now focusing on displaying the correct URLs for Google Console configuration. I\'ll show the dynamic Development URL, which is `https://ais-dev-fxev6c4fuqgsv4djn5yx3g-45527719759.us-east1.run.app`, and the Shared URL, which is `https://ais-pre-fxev6c4fuqgsv4djn5yx3g-45527719759.us-east1.run.app`. I\'ll also ensure that the `window.google` object is correctly typed, either by declaring an interface or casting `window as any` for type safety. I\'m exploring the nuances of `npm run lint` and the `window.google` object.\n\n\n**Adding Copy Buttons**\n\nI\'m thrilled with the progress! I\'m adding copy buttons to the configuration panel to provide a user-friendly experience. Now, users can easily copy and paste the dynamic URLs for their Google Console configuration, simplifying the setup process for authentic Google sign-in. I\'ve confirmed that both `tobiasarraiza17@gmail.com` and `totoarr17@gmail.com` are admin accounts, providing a seamless testing experience. I\'m focusing on polishing the GoogleSignIn file for a top-tier visual experience.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will replace the contents of `/src/components/GoogleSignIn.tsx` to support the genuine Google Identity Services SDK, render the native Google Sign-In button, and provide a developer tools manager so the Google client can be configured with the proper Google Cloud credentials or bypassed with safe simulation profiles."
